WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.354
Go's HTTP ecosystem is practical and
direct,

2
00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:04.337
which makes service boundaries easier to
see.

3
00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:06.954
A handler receives a request, checks
method and content type,

4
00:00:07.034 --> 00:00:09.669
enforces size limits, parses input,
authenticates the caller,

5
00:00:09.749 --> 00:00:13.165
authorizes the action, performs work, logs
useful events, and returns a response.

6
00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:16.270
Security improves when that order is
consistent across the application.

7
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.862
Request limits should happen early.

8
00:00:17.942 --> 00:00:20.431
Servers and handlers need sensible read
and write timeouts,

9
00:00:20.511 --> 00:00:23.878
maximum header sizes where appropriate,
body size limits, content-type checks,

10
00:00:23.958 --> 00:00:24.836
and bounded parsing.

11
00:00:24.916 --> 00:00:27.258
Without limits, a service can spend too
much memory, CPU,

12
00:00:27.338 --> 00:00:29.143
or time before reaching the business
logic.

13
00:00:29.223 --> 00:00:32.053
Authentication and authorization should be
separate in the design.

14
00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:33.792
Authentication identifies the caller.

15
00:00:33.872 --> 00:00:37.288
Authorization decides whether that caller
may perform the action on the requested

16
00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:37.807
resource.

17
00:00:37.887 --> 00:00:39.644
Middleware can help with shared concerns,

18
00:00:39.724 --> 00:00:42.944
but resource-level decisions often need
trusted data loaded by the handler or

19
00:00:43.024 --> 00:00:43.658
service layer.

20
00:00:43.738 --> 00:00:46.617
Middleware is useful for consistent
logging, recovery, request IDs,

21
00:00:46.697 --> 00:00:49.722
size limits, security headers where
relevant, and common policy checks.

22
00:00:49.802 --> 00:00:51.217
It should remain understandable.

23
00:00:51.297 --> 00:00:54.615
A reviewer should be able to tell which
controls run for a route and which controls

24
00:00:54.695 --> 00:00:56.061
must still happen in the handler.

25
00:00:56.141 --> 00:00:58.727
CORS should be deliberate and narrow for
browser-facing APIs.

26
00:00:58.807 --> 00:01:02.174
Safe error responses should help callers
recover without exposing stack traces,

27
00:01:02.254 --> 00:01:05.182
internal queries, private configuration,
or sensitive identifiers.

28
00:01:05.262 --> 00:01:07.799
Service-to-service boundaries should
document ownership,

29
00:01:07.879 --> 00:01:11.343
expected callers, authentication method,
authorization rule, and data contract.

30
00:01:11.423 --> 00:01:13.082
When those boundaries are written down,

31
00:01:13.162 --> 00:01:16.334
new routes and clients can be reviewed
against an existing pattern instead of

32
00:01:16.414 --> 00:01:17.780
inventing trust rules each time.
